>> title: Onion Rings
>> category: Fried & Crispy
>> tags: onion, fried, beer batter, side, stovetop
>> counters: Diner
>> aka: onion rings, beer battered onion rings, fried onion rings, frizzled onions
>> servings: 4
>> time: 50 min
>> slack: narrow — the batter is used immediately; left to stand it goes smooth and slack and fries into a shell that slips off the onion

>> step: cut in 1/2-in rings, soak 30 min
Soak @large yellow onions{2}(cut in 1/2-in rings, pushed apart) in @buttermilk{2%cup}(480 mL) ~soak{30%min}, which takes the raw bite off and gives the batter something to grip.

>> step: whisk the batter, lumps and all
Whisk @all-purpose flour{1%cup}(120 g), @cornstarch{1/2%cup}(60 g), @baking powder{1%tsp}(4 g), @kosher salt{1%tsp}(6 g) and @sweet paprika{1/2%tsp}(1 g) with @cold lager{1%cup}(240 mL) to the thickness of pouring cream, leaving the lumps.

>> step: dip, drain a second, fry 3 min at 365°F (185°C)
Dip @&(~2)soaked rings{} one at a time into @&(~1)beer batter{}, let the drip run off for a second, and fry in @peanut oil{2%qt}(1.9 L) at 365°F (185°C) ~fry{3%min}, turning once, until pale gold — they darken on the rack.

>> step: drain standing up, salt hot
Drain @&(~1)onion rings{} on a rack rather than paper, standing them on edge, and salt them hot with @flaky salt{1%tsp}(4 g).
